# qBTMCP - Cursor Rules

## 📚 RULE #1: Check Central Documentation First

**BEFORE making any changes, ALWAYS check:**

- **Central Docs:** `D:\Dev\repos\mcp-central-docs\`
- **Standards:** `mcp-central-docs/STANDARDS.md` - Documentation & testing standards
- **FastMCP Guide:** `mcp-central-docs/FASTMCP_2.12_MIGRATION.md` - Framework standards  
- **MCPB Packaging:** `mcp-central-docs/MCPB_PACKAGING_STANDARDS.md` - Package standards

**Why central docs?** Single source of truth for all MCP projects. Prevents duplication and ensures consistency.

---

## Development Standards

### Code Quality
- Use type hints for all function/method signatures
- Follow PEP 8 style guide with Ruff
- Maximum function length: 50 lines
- Maximum class length: 300 lines
- Minimum test coverage: 80%
- Use docstrings for all public modules, classes, and functions

### Error Handling
- Use specific exception types
- Include context in error messages
- Log errors before raising when appropriate
- Use custom exception classes for domain-specific errors

### FastMCP 2.12 Standards
- Use `@mcp.tool` decorator with only `name` and `description` parameters
- No `inputSchema` or `outputSchema` in tool definitions
- Tools should return standard Python types (dict, list, str, etc.)
- Use async/await for all tool functions
- Proper error handling with try/catch blocks

### File Organization
- Keep files under 300 lines
- Group related functionality in modules
- Use clear, descriptive names
- Follow the established directory structure:
  ```
  src/qbtmcp/
  ├── services/          # Business logic services
  ├── tools/             # MCP tool definitions
  ├── models/            # Data models
  └── utils/             # Utility functions
  ```

### Testing
- Write tests for new features
- Maintain good test coverage
- Run tests before merging to main
- Use pytest with async support

### Documentation
- Document public APIs and interfaces
- Keep README files updated
- Document environment setup
- Maintain CHANGELOG.md
- Document architectural decisions

### Security
- Never hardcode credentials
- Use environment variables for sensitive data
- Follow principle of least privilege
- Keep dependencies up to date
- Audit for known vulnerabilities

### PowerShell Scripts
- Use proper PowerShell syntax
- Include error handling with `-ErrorAction Stop`
- Use `New-Item -ItemType Directory -Force` for directory creation
- Use `Test-Path` to check file/directory existence
- Use `Get-Content -Path` for file operations

### Git Workflow
- Use semantic versioning (SemVer)
- Write clear commit messages
- Use feature branches for new development
- Require pull request reviews
- Run CI/CD checks before merging

### Dependencies
- Use UV for dependency management
- Pin dependency versions in pyproject.toml
- Regular security audits with Safety
- Document all third-party dependencies

### Performance
- Profile before optimizing
- Optimize for readability first
- Consider memory usage and execution time
- Use async/await for I/O operations

### Change Management
- Never remove, rewrite, or break major functionality without explicit user instruction
- Always clarify and confirm before making breaking changes
- Provide explicit diffs and summaries for all major edits
- Follow user-specified workflow preferences strictly
- Set up safety nets like auto-staged commits when possible

### Rule Verification
- Add "lgr1 " to cascade outputs to prove rules were read
